# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files for more about ignoring files.
#
# If you find yourself ignoring temporary files generated by your text editor
# or operating system, you probably want to add a global ignore instead:
#   git config --global core.excludesfile '~/.gitignore_global'

# Ignore bundler config.
/.bundle

# Ignore all logfiles and tempfiles.
/log/*
/tmp/*
!/log/.keep
!/tmp/.keep

# Ignore Byebug command history file.
.byebug_history

config/postal.yml
config/smtp.cert
config/smtp.key
config/signing.key
config/postal/**/*

spec/config/postal.local.yml

public/assets
vendor/bundle

Procfile.local
VERSION
BRANCH

.rubocop-https*

# `.env*` has no slash in it, so it matches at every level of the tree -- which
# is what stops deploy/.env, holding the secrets, from being committed. It also
# catches deploy/.env.example, which is not a secret and must be committed:
# ./deploy.sh init copies it to make .env, so a fresh clone without it has
# nothing to copy and no record of which variables exist. The negation below
# works only because no parent directory of it is excluded.
.env*
!.env.example
!deploy/.env.example

# Patterns above which contain a slash are anchored to this directory, so the
# config/ entries do not reach the equivalent paths under deploy/. These are the
# deployment host's own copies and they are the most sensitive files here:
# signing.key is the DKIM private key, and anything Postal signs is verifiable
# against its public half.
/deploy/config/signing.key
/deploy/config/smtp.cert
/deploy/config/smtp.key

# The ACME client's state directory, written by ./deploy.sh tls. Holds the
# account private key, every issued certificate and key, and the per-domain
# configuration acme.sh renews from. Losing it means losing the ability to renew
# rather than the certificates themselves, but the account key is a credential
# and the private keys under it are the SMTP server's.
/deploy/acme/

# Rendered by ./deploy.sh init from .env, so it contains the application
# database password in a CREATE USER statement. The MariaDB image runs this
# directory on first initialization of an empty volume.
/deploy/database/

# ./deploy.sh backup writes one archive per run here, and each archive contains a
# dump of every schema, config/signing.key and the whole of .env. This is the
# single most sensitive path in the repository, and it defaults to living inside
# the working tree, which is why it has to be named explicitly.
/deploy/backups/

# Written by ./deploy.sh upgrade and read by ./deploy.sh rollback. Records the
# revision this host was on before the last upgrade. Per-host state, not source.
/deploy/.upgrade-state

node_modules
yarn.lock

